          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and it is starting to interfere with daily life. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line treatment, and where does it sit in ICD-10-CM classification?",
          "answer": "If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, seek medical advice [diagnostic]. If you are experiencing anxiety that interferes with daily life, you should consult a healthcare professional [consultation].\n\nAnxiety is often assessed using screening tools and questionnaires, which typically prompt a diagnostic evaluation [diagnostic, research]. One common tool is the seven-item Generalized Anxiety Disorder Questionnaire (GAD-7), used for screening and tracking symptom severity [diagnostic]. Scores of 5 indicate mild anxiety, 10 moderate, and 15 severe [diagnostic]. A score of 10 or higher usually indicates the need for further assessment [diagnostic, research]. This evaluation also includes ruling out physical causes such as thyroid problems or arrhythmia, reviewing the effects of substances, caffeine, and medications, and checking for co-occurring depression [research].\n\nRegarding first-line treatment for generalized anxiety disorder, clinical guidance from preventive services and psychiatric associations recommends two options with similar effectiveness, chosen in discussion with the individual: Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) and pharmacotherapy [research]. CBT can be provided individually, in groups, or through guided digital programs, and has the strongest evidence among psychological therapies [research]. Pharmacotherapy involves medication, specifically a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) or a serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) [research]. The diagnostic specialist notes that self-management approaches with evidence include structured exercise, relaxation, mindfulness practice, and guided digital programs [diagnostic].\n\nIn the US clinical modification of the International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10-CM), anxiety disorders are typically found in Chapter 5, which covers Mental, Behavioral, and Neurodevelopmental disorders (F01-F99) [consultation, diagnostic]. This chapter includes codes from F40 to F48 [diagnostic]. For instance, generalized anxiety disorder is classified under F41.1, unspecified anxiety disorder is F41.9, panic disorder is F41.0, social anxiety disorder is F40.10, and specific phobias are F40.2xx [consultation].\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
